<details open>
    <summary>What does this site do?</summary>
    <p>
        You describe what you are looking for in plain language. That can be a research
        question, a topic, or the outline of a literature section. An AI research assistant then plans a search
        strategy, runs it against a large database of economics papers, and writes a short
        literature review from what it finds. Every paper it mentions is shown as a card with
        its abstract and a link to the original source.
    </p>
    <p>
        A run typically takes a few minutes. You can watch the assistant work through its steps,
        and the result can be shared via a link or exported as Markdown, BibTeX, Excel or PDF.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>How is this different from the regular semantic search?</summary>
    <p>
        The <a href="https://econpapers.eduard-bruell.de">semantic search</a> runs a single
        query and returns a ranked list, which is fast and great when you know roughly what you want.
        Agentic Search is for the messier cases: it can break a brief into several searches,
        follow citations, filter by what you asked for in plain language, and then write up what
        it found as a structured review instead of a list.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Can I trust what the review says?</summary>
    <p>
        Only partly, so please read this one carefully. The review text is written by a large
        language model, and like all such models it can <strong>hallucinate</strong>: it may
        misread a paper from its abstract, overstate or garble a finding, or draw connections
        that are not in the original work.
    </p>
    <p>
        What you <em>can</em> rely on: the papers themselves are real. They come directly from
        the database, every card links to the original source, and the citation data is taken
        from RePEc, not generated.
    </p>
    <p>
        Treat the review as a fast first map of a literature, not as a substitute for reading
        the papers. Verify any claim against the linked original before you cite or repeat it.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Where does the data come from?</summary>
    <p>
        The paper database is built from <a href="https://repec.org">RePEc</a>, covering a
        curated subset of roughly 250 major economics journals and working paper series with
        abstracts and citation data. It is the same database that powers the semantic search,
        and it is updated about once per month. Very recent papers may not be in it yet.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Why is there a password?</summary>
    <p>
        Each run costs real money in AI processing. While the service is in preview, access is
        limited to invited testers. If you would like access, email
        <a href="mailto:eduard.bruell@zew.de">eduard.bruell@zew.de</a>.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>What is stored when I run a search?</summary>
    <p>
        Unlike the regular semantic search, this site stores your task description in full.
        It has to, so that share links can replay the whole run. A run record contains:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>The task description you typed (and any clarification answers)</li>
        <li>The steps the assistant took and the papers it found</li>
        <li>The generated review text</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Anyone with the share link can see all of this, so don't put anything confidential or
        personal in a task description. To generate the review, the task description and
        intermediate results are also sent to external AI model providers for processing
        (see the data protection section below).
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>My run failed or stopped halfway. What now?</summary>
    <p>
        Runs occasionally fail, usually because an AI model timed out or returned something
        unusable. Just run the search again. If a run keeps failing on the same brief, email
        <a href="mailto:eduard.bruell@zew.de">eduard.bruell@zew.de</a> with the brief text.
    </p>
</details>

<details>
    <summary>Who can I contact if something looks wrong?</summary>
    <p>
        Please send corrections, feedback or examples of bad reviews to
        <a href="mailto:eduard.bruell@zew.de">eduard.bruell@zew.de</a>.
    </p>
</details>


